<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcha Real</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first printed <a href="/years/1761">1761</a>; Honour March <a href="/years/1770">1770</a>; official version <a href="/years/1908">1908</a> and <a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The &quot;Marcha Real&quot; (Royal March), officially the &quot;Marcha Real Española&quot; and also called the &quot;Marcha Granadera&quot; (Grenadier March), is the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, one of the oldest in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcha-real.jpg" alt="Marcha Real" width="330" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manuel de Espinosa de los Monteros, Bartolomé Pérez Casas, Francisco Grau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Marcha_Granadera_(1761).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Marcha Real is among the oldest national anthems in the world. It was first printed in a document dated 1761, Manuel de Espinosa's Book of the Ordinance of Newly Played Military Fife and Drum Calls by The Spanish Infantry, where it is called La Marcha Granadera, the Grenadier March, and Manuel de Espinosa de los Monteros is named as composer. In 1861 a periodical claimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick II of Prussia</a> had composed it, and in 1864 Col. Antonio Vallecillo said he had given it to the Count of Colomera, who was learning Prussian tactics by order of King Charles III. This story, which entered the Enciclopedia Espasa in 1908, is spurious.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In 1770 Charles III declared the Marcha de Granaderos the official Honor March, formalising its use in public on solemn occasions. It became the official Spanish anthem during the reign of Queen Isabella II. After the 1868 Revolution General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-prim">Juan Prim</a> organised a contest for a new anthem in 1870, and over 400 compositions were submitted, but the jury advised that the Marcha de Granaderos was already regarded as the official anthem, and the contest was suspended. A Royal Circular Order of 27 August 1908 made the score orchestrated by Bartolomé Pérez Casas the official version.</p><p>The present version was written by Francisco Grau and has been official since the Royal Decree of 10 October 1997, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> bought the author rights from Pérez Casas's heirs for 130 million pesetas. It is sixteen bars long, in two sections of four repeated bars each, in B-flat major, and lasts 52 seconds. The full version is the honours music for the King.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Marcha Real is one of only three national anthems, with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, with no official lyrics. Words have been written for it, including verses by Eduardo Marquina for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> wedding anniversary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a>, a Carlist version, and lyrics by José María Pemán adapted to the ideology of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a>, but none was made official. During the Second Republic the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego</a> was the republic's anthem. The Marcha Real has been played without words since 1978.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marcha Real” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In October 1823 Ferdinand overthrew the liberal government and repealed the constitution, and the <b>Marcha Real</b> became the national anthem again.</blockquote><a href="/songs/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego</a></li><li><blockquote>Felipe said he had based it on three pieces: the &quot;<b>Marcha Real</b>&quot; of Spain, the Grand March from Giuseppe Verdi's Aida and &quot;La Marseillaise&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang (Chosen Land)</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;<b>Marcha Real</b>&quot; (Royal March), officially the &quot;Marcha Real Española&quot; and also called the &quot;Marcha Granadera&quot; (Grenadier March), is the national anthem of Spain, one of the oldest in the world.</blockquote><a href="/songs/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a></li></ul></section>
